@page "/learn/virtualize-variable-height"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Lists with items of any height · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Lists with items of any height</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your thread mixes one-line log entries with long emails. Can you virtualize it without the jumps?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Grids &amp; lists · Interactive Server · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> nothing to write. <code>Virtualize</code> itself was fixed: it averages every
        height it has measured, and keeps what you're reading still when items above it grow. .NET 10 virtualized
        the same markup, but the scrollbar jumped and expanding moved the page.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a long support thread: short replies mixed with long emails, so the items vary a lot in height.
        Long messages start cut to three lines, with <b>Show more</b> on each and <b>Expand all</b> above the list.
    </p>
    <p>
        .NET 11 fixed this inside <code>Virtualize</code>, so the old list can't run on this site. Fig. 1 is a video
        of our .NET 10 repro: a small standalone app with the same <code>Virtualize</code> markup as Fig. 2, but its
        own sample thread (a sync bot's log lines mixed with long emails) and plain styling, so it looks different. The dark
        strip along the bottom of the video is the same readout script that runs under the live list in Fig. 2.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The thread on .NET 10, recorded" Url="demo/variable-height/incident/net10"
                Label=".NET 10 · recorded" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            A recording, not a live list: it plays by itself on a loop, and its controls pause or rewind it. It opens
            on an empty box; the messages and the readout, a dark strip along the bottom of the video, appear a moment
            later. The capture was narrower than the repro's page, so long lines are cut off at its edge: that's the
            video, not the bug. As it scrolls down 24 times, watch the readout: <b>Scroll range</b> keeps changing, so
            the scrollbar thumb resizes and jumps. By the end it has swung by <b>13,191px</b>. Then it presses <b>Expand all</b>, and the
            message being read is pushed <b>274px</b> down. Both totals are also printed under the video.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>It guessed from the dozen items in view</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Virtualize</code> only renders the items near the view. It stands in for the rest with two spacers,
        sized from an average height. In .NET 10 that average came from the items rendered at that moment:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">How .NET 10 sized the messages you can't see</span>
        <pre class="snip">in view: mostly log lines    <span class="dim">small average</span>  → range <span class="err">shrinks</span>
scroll on: a few emails      <span class="dim">large average</span>  → range <span class="err">grows</span>
scroll on: log lines again   <span class="dim">small average</span>  → range <span class="err">shrinks</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        Every new mix of short and long messages resized the whole scroll range. That's the 13,191px swing in Fig. 1.
        A scripted run of the same repro saw a similar swing (12,550px), and while it scrolled steadily down, the
        message at the top of the box changed to an earlier one 10 times: the list jumped back up.
    </p>
    <p>
        .NET 10 also set <code>overflow-anchor: none</code> on the scrolling box. That turns off the browser's scroll
        anchoring, so when a message above you grew, everything below it, including what you were reading, was
        pushed down.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>The same Virtualize, on .NET 11</h2>
    <p>There's no new API. Give the list a scrolling box with a height, and let messages be their natural size.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole list</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;div class="thread"&gt;   <span class="dim">@@* height: 440px; overflow-y: auto *@@</span>
    &lt;Virtualize Items="messages" Context="m" ItemSize="80"&gt;
        &lt;article class="msg"&gt;...&lt;/article&gt;
    &lt;/Virtualize&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        Nothing in it is highlighted, because nothing in it changes. <code>ItemSize</code> stays, but it's only a
        first guess now. .NET 11 keeps a running average of every item it has measured, so the range settles instead of swinging. It also leaves scroll anchoring on, with the spacers
        excluded, so the anchor is always a real message. Here's the same thread, live.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same thread, live on .NET 11" Url="demo/variable-height/incident"
                Label=".NET 11 · live">
        <Caption>
            The readout under the list says <b>scroll to measure</b> until you scroll. Drag the list's scrollbar down,
            then back up: the <b>swung</b> figure stays a small fraction of Fig. 1's 13,191px, and the thumb barely
            moves. Then scroll to the middle and press <b>Expand all</b>. The readout says how far the top message
            moved: next to nothing (0 or 1px), though the long messages above it have opened.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Both figures use the same <code>Virtualize</code> markup, over different threads. Here's Fig. 2's:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/VariableHeight/IncidentThread.razor" Feature="virtualize-variable-height">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's thread; the repro behind Fig. 1 has the same <code>Virtualize</code> line. That line is
            highlighted to show where the behaviour lives, not because you change it: its comment says it's unchanged
            since .NET 10. The difference is how .NET 11 measures and anchors.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>One height for everything, or no virtualizing</h2>
    <p>To keep the list still, apps gave up either the natural heights or the virtualizing:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways round it in .NET 10, and what they cost</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;Virtualize Items="messages" <span class="err">ItemSize="120"</span>&gt;
    <span class="dim">@@* 120px, overflow: hidden *@@</span>
    &lt;article class="preview"&gt;...
        &lt;button&gt;Read all&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/Virtualize&gt;
&lt;MessageDialog Message="open" /&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">every item is the height Virtualize expects, so nothing jumps</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a one-liner gets an email's box, and long messages are read in a dialog</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;button @@onclick="() =&gt; shown += 50"&gt;
    Show 50 older messages
&lt;/button&gt;
@@foreach (var m in messages.<span class="err">TakeLast(shown)</span>)</pre>
                <span class="why">nothing is virtualized, so heights can be anything</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ every message you load stays on the page, so a long thread still gets slow</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>It measures, and it holds your place</h2>
    <p>
        The repro's own files, built for .NET 11, swung by under 1,000px (34,115 to 35,067px), against 12,550px in
        the scripted .NET 10 run, and the top message never jumped back. Expand all moved the message being read by 1px. Fig. 2 shows the same on
        this site's thread.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you used the first workaround, keep <code>ItemSize</code> but stop treating it as a fixed height: remove
        the fixed-height box, its <code>overflow: hidden</code> and the dialog, and let messages be their natural size.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>One thing changes on upgrade:</b> more items render by default. <code>OverscanCount</code> now defaults to
        15 either side, up from 3. If your items are expensive to render, set it back to 3. QuickGrid still defaults
        to 3.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Give it a scrolling box.</b> <code>Virtualize</code> scrolls inside the nearest parent with <code>overflow-y: auto</code> or <code>scroll</code>. Give that box a fixed height, like the demo's 440px.</li>
        <li><b>Close, not exact.</b> The range still drifts a little as it learns, and wildly varied heights are estimated less well.</li>
        <li><b>Refreshing starts the measuring again.</b> <code>RefreshDataAsync()</code> and changing <code>ItemSize</code> both reset the average.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
